my name is Kayla ward I went from  ministry and the church to finally  living my truth as a trans woman who was  going to try out with the WNBA and this  is that story I honestly don't remember  the first time I picked up a basketball  but I do remember having one of those  little tight goals those plastic ones  and my mom will go out there and shoot  around with me I was probably like free  three or four and I remember getting  that first point where I was like would  run up and dunked on it and I broke it  and landed on my head but that didn't  deter me like I loved it I was so  excited that I was able to do that from  there I would sneak away into the inner  city when I was young as seven and I  would play basketball and it's just  where I would go every day I was down  every day that the thoughts wouldn't  leave my mind of how I felt inside my  earliest memories of really feeling like  it wasn't this boy as everybody said I  was was when I was around five or six I  remember in particular probably a little  graphic but going to take a bath one day  and just really not liking what was on  my body I really wanted to take a knife  and cut it off it was just something  that was telling me that this wasn't  right and as I got older and I was  around more people especially other  girls I was like that's supposed to be  me and it wasn't necessarily playing  with dolls or the dresses cuz I'm still  not very girly I'm not very feminine I  don't wear a lot of makeup I'd rather be  on a basketball court so it's it's hard  to say what moment was really that  trigger it was just a feeling inside  that told me who I was  it was difficult feeling like you didn't  belong or fit in so it's a lot about  putting on a front I was an amazing  actor for 30 years especially as a kid  because kids are very intuitive they  pick up on things  just why I really push so hard to sports  it's hard to really pinpoint something  different about a jock I guess how I  related was just through sports and then  eventually through religion I joined a  youth group at church found new friends  and every time the doors to the church  were open I was there I was involved in  every every activity every Bible study  just looking for answers I found myself  really praying hard for the feelings I  had internally to go away I felt a sense  of belonging that I'd never felt before  with the church and the family that I  had so I decided I wanted to give my  life over and make that a career went to  about this college to pursue youth  ministry or mission work while I was  doing this mission work I did find  somebody that I fell in love with they  became my best friend  and I thought this is just God's  direction everything's going according  to plan I'm gonna be this wonderful  husband I'll eventually be a father  and it'll all work out we were together  for 10 years married for seven the last  three years of the marriage kind of  started to fall apart  there was there was division between my  wife and I it was kind of dressing on  the side and trying to come to terms  with myself and finding community on  mine she would almost catch me every  time and I would lie about it so I  remember her asking for a divorce and it  broke me  we were Christian we what we were  married I'm just told that's to be part  we weren't supposed to get divorced so I  begged her to stay I said we'll try  counseling whatever she said it's not  gonna happen when she moved out it's  kind of when I really hit  I was done I really was I didn't want to  put anybody else through what I felt  like I've heard through I didn't feel my  parents would accept her understand so I  thought what else do I have to lose I  might as well just end it so I had a  bottle of pills but I was just gonna  down it was night and the house was dark  and I was just crying my face was in my  hands and in my pillow and I was scared  to do it but I was ready and my cats  they would never be near each other but  they both in that moment we're climbing  up on my lap trying to push my hands  away they were licking the tears one on  each side and it  it made it even harder because I was so  ready but I didn't want to lose my  babies their attention and love to me is  what really kind of kept me here not  feeling alone was probably the most  important part  I threw the bottle of pills away and it  was the next day that I started seeing a  gender therapist started hormones and  began loving my truth I called my dad  crying and really scared because he  always called me his butt and told me  how proud he was of me and they want to  lose that and he assured me that he left  me no matter what I was his kid and that  when I remarried one day he would walk  me down the aisle so that was reassuring  it really helped move me forward know  when I called my mom completely  different story she was pissed so we had  an argument I told her she couldn't use  the right pronouns or call me by the  right name or even just say my child  that I wouldn't be able to talk to her  I'd have to kind of cut that out and so  we didn't talk for two years and it  wasn't until I visited again where she  introduced me to her co-workers as her  daughter so it took some time and I  learned a lot too that that sometimes  we're not the only ones transitioning  those around us our friends and our  family are going through the same thing  it's a shock to them and they kind of  have to come along the journey with you  and it's not always gonna be smooth  sailing I lost a lot of the friends  initially that we're connected to me and  my ex-wife we did go to a Baptist  College together so everybody really  disagreed with who I am my views of the  church now are completely different I  still have this personal faith the  church today is problematic and stepping  away from it allowed me to see that more  they're completely missing the point  the Bible specifically talks a lot about  love and most people have no idea what  that really means or how to provide that  to other people  it took a little time to come out at  work but I was actually surprised a lot  of people are very accepting and  supportive and pushed me a lot to break  out of my comfort zone and stop being so  shy and nervous moved to Dallas Texas  for a few years I met a lot of amazing  people which that turn led to LA because  I really wanted to pursue my passion in  2021 I want to try out for the WNBA so  unfortunately a lot of leagues don't  have guidelines that allow trans people  to play however the International  Olympic Committee does have a policy if  you are a trans woman you have to be  living in your true gender for at least  a year you have to be on HRT hormone  replacement for at least a year and you  have to test consistently for 12 months  at a testosterone level of 10 or below  they are changing that testosterone  level to 5 or below because cisgender  women usually range between one and two  some can produce higher so for those who  consistently say that it's not an even  playing field when say a trans woman  joins assists women's league I  transitioned seven years ago I don't  produce testosterone I lost a lot of  muscle mass so if I were to compete  against men now especially in a  professional level I would just get  demolished and thrown around the court  like a rag doll when it comes to trans  people in sports it honestly breaks down  to almost a case by case scenario  because there are requirements of living  in your gender for a year and have  been on HRT for a year so as long as  those things are in place I would say  trans people should be allowed to play  the sport of their choosing in the  gender that matches her identity I have  no problems hearing opposing views  because it's important at the dialogue  Sports is about community it's about  connecting it's about not feeling  isolated alone it provides this sense of  belonging and so everywhere from little  kids all the way up to adults we need  that if I'm honest I have a better  chance of winning a lottery while being  struck by lightning and I say that not  because of my skill level but I'm 37 so  by the time I try out I'll be 39 so not  exactly young and I've blown out of me  I've damaged my back I've had  concussions so my body may not let me  play but it's not the only goal I have  if I don't get to make it if I can  create the policy that allows other  people to follow their dreams that's a  win when I grew up we didn't have the  internet we didn't have social media  there was no trans representation on TV  for as long as I could remember I felt  like there was just something wrong with  me I was a freak so now seeing so many  people living their lives out and proud  that means everything I have people all  over probably more or less the Midwest  in the south who reach out who  understand my story because they're  married or they're in a relationship  they're in the church they have kids and  they feel trapped so I get to help  provide that vision of what life can be  if they just allow themselves to be  for me life is just beautiful when you  aren't having to hide who you are so I  can say the biggest difference between  before and now is I'm free if I could  talk to my younger self I would tell  that little kid that it's okay you may  not understand everything now you may  feel afraid and alone but I promise you  you're not life can be beautiful it can  be worth living you can make friends you  don't have to be alone and you'd be  surprised at how many people will  actually accept you and love you as who  you are so you have to let go of that  self hate because you think it's gonna  come from people outside and it's not  going to not the way you think most of  that comes from yourself so you have to  let that go breathe and let yourself be  [Music]
 

